  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 3.1. Increased capacity of member States to formulate and implement a new generation of gender-responsive national employment policies, including for youth</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The National Employment Strategy (NES), adopted in November 2020 has a strategic objective to achieve full, productive and freely chosen employment for jobseekers, particularly youth and women. It includes key policy interventions aiming at promoting investment in human capital and increasing youth employment opportunities, including:

1) Private sector resilience and ability to increase productivity while creating decent employment opportunities for jobseekers, particularly youth and women to reduce youth unemployment

2) Improve alignment of education and higher education with labour market needs to enhance skills and employability of youth.

3) Strengthen labour market governance and develop active labour market policies and programmes, ensuring stronger linkages and more efficient alignment between supply and demand sides with focus on improving employment services for young job seekers, and improving complementarity between employment and social protection programmes for youth not in employment, education or training (NEET). The ILO&apos;s Employment diagnostic provided youth-specific recommendations based on an evidence-based analysis of the situation of the youth in the labour market in the OPT. Similarly, the study on the COVID-19 impact takes into account the specificities of the youth.

The ILO, throughout the drafting process of the policy as well as the consultations organized with national stakeholders, ensured inclusion of the youth and the identification of policy measures for better youth employment outcomes</narrative>
   </description>
